1
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:07,260
Good afternoon. We are Gabriel, Jonathan, Thomas and Ziad.

2
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:11,030
We started this work with a simple question:

3
00:00:11,080 --> 00:00:15,460
will it rain tomorrow at a weather station in Australia?

4
00:00:15,980 --> 00:00:21,210
Training a model for that is the easy part, so today we want to show you

5
00:00:21,260 --> 00:00:23,460
everything around it.

6
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:27,550
I will cover the architecture and the problem, talk a little about drift

7
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:29,450
and also our gateway.

8
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:35,330
After me, Jonathan and Thomas take you through the rest, and Ziad ends

9
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:39,760
with a live demonstration of our system.

10
00:00:41,060 --> 00:00:43,700
Let's start with the big picture.

11
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:47,410
The whole system runs in Docker Compose.

12
00:00:47,460 --> 00:00:53,210
It all begins with data: the Kaggle

13
00:00:53,260 --> 00:00:59,740
Australian weather history, plus new days from the Open-Meteo API.

14
00:01:00,180 --> 00:01:06,660
They are in the same format, so we use both of them.

15
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:12,650
To keep the system running on its own, we use Airflow,

16
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:18,370
which runs two pipelines: a daily one, and one that retrains

17
00:01:18,420 --> 00:01:22,160
the model whenever required.

18
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:28,470
The data lives in Supabase Postgres, dataset versions in DVC, and all the

19
00:01:28,520 --> 00:01:30,980
secrets in Supabase Vault.

20
00:01:31,560 --> 00:01:33,040
When we train,

21
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:39,230
MLflow records the run, and a new model only becomes champion if it

22
00:01:39,280 --> 00:01:41,550
passes our quality gate.

23
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:46,830
Users reach the champion through

24
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:52,370
Nginx and FastAPI, and a new champion goes live

25
00:01:52,420 --> 00:01:56,680
without the need to restart the system.

26
00:01:57,220 --> 00:01:59,970
Once it's live, we keep watching it.

27
00:02:00,020 --> 00:02:05,500
We use Grafana for the API, and Evidently for drift.

28
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:11,330
What closes the loop at the bottom: when the real

29
00:02:11,380 --> 00:02:18,260
weather comes in and recall falls below 0.75,

30
00:02:18,760 --> 00:02:23,160
Grafana triggers a retrain of the model.

31
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,910
So why do we need all this?

32
00:02:27,960 --> 00:02:33,170
Because the problem is harder than it looks.

33
00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:42,360
For each prediction, we take one station on one day, with 20 weather

34
00:02:42,410 --> 00:02:49,310
measurements, and we return the chance of rain tomorrow.

35
00:02:49,630 --> 00:02:53,480
The tricky part is that only 23% of days are rainy.

36
00:02:53,530 --> 00:02:58,930
In other words, a model that always says no

37
00:02:59,650 --> 00:03:06,030
is 77% accurate, and never catches a rainy day.

38
00:03:06,250 --> 00:03:10,300
So we don't judge on accuracy.

39
00:03:10,350 --> 00:03:18,610
We pick the model on ROC AUC, and ours scores 0.87.

40
00:03:19,930 --> 00:03:25,540
Recall also has to stay above 0.75, because

41
00:03:25,590 --> 00:03:31,310
missing a rainy day hurts more than a false alarm.

42
00:03:33,250 --> 00:03:38,510
Then we asked: what happens when the weather changes?

43
00:03:38,670 --> 00:03:44,580
So we took the model to Singapore, which is much rainier but has

44
00:03:44,630 --> 00:03:46,470
the same data format.

45
00:03:46,970 --> 00:03:52,560
It rains there on 83% of days, and Evidently

46
00:03:52,610 --> 00:03:56,070
flags all five features we monitor.

47
00:03:57,110 --> 00:04:00,690
The model suffers.

48
00:04:01,210 --> 00:04:07,590
ROC AUC drops from 0.87 to 0.70.

49
00:04:09,230 --> 00:04:17,650
Retraining on Singapore data helps, from 0.64 up to 0.70,

50
00:04:18,290 --> 00:04:22,350
but it only wins back part of the loss.

51
00:04:23,010 --> 00:04:29,310
That's why we need to keep monitoring the system.

52
00:04:30,170 --> 00:04:35,040
One last piece concerns security.

53
00:04:35,090 --> 00:04:38,190
Nginx is the only way in.

54
00:04:38,410 --> 00:04:43,610
Every other service only listens inside the machine.

55
00:04:47,490 --> 00:04:53,760
Each request passes three checks: TLS,

56
00:04:53,810 --> 00:04:58,430
a rate limit per IP address, and a login in FastAPI.

57
00:04:59,090 --> 00:05:05,470
Six live tests in our CI check these layers.

58
00:05:06,830 --> 00:05:12,460
And with that, I will hand over to Jonathan, who will show you how we version

59
00:05:12,510 --> 00:05:15,810
the data and the model.

60
00:05:16,110 --> 00:05:18,550
Jonathan, over to you.

61
00:05:19,290 --> 00:05:22,830
Looking at the data, we have two different sources.

62
00:05:23,110 --> 00:05:29,020
On the one hand, we have Kaggle, which gives us 145,000 rows for

63
00:05:29,070 --> 00:05:33,250
49 weather stations, covering 2007 to 2017.

64
00:05:34,010 --> 00:05:39,640
On the other hand, we have Open-Meteo, whose API lets us get data from more

65
00:05:39,690 --> 00:05:42,790
recent years, and even from today.

66
00:05:43,370 --> 00:05:45,160
It gives us the same 23 columns,

67
00:05:45,210 --> 00:05:50,260
so it's really convenient.

68
00:05:50,310 --> 00:05:52,950
We can merge everything into one big dataset.

69
00:05:53,470 --> 00:05:57,440
We then preprocess it and split it by date, because we are of course

70
00:05:57,490 --> 00:05:59,620
working with predictions for the future.

71
00:05:59,670 --> 00:06:03,530
We don't want data leakage from the future into the past.

72
00:06:04,530 --> 00:06:09,810
Every dataset that is generated gets its own unique identifier,

73
00:06:10,270 --> 00:06:14,350
so we get a manifest with a SHA-256 of each file.

74
00:06:15,130 --> 00:06:20,290
Data versioning then happens in DVC.

75
00:06:22,300 --> 00:06:27,600
Which dataset is currently in use is also passed to MLflow,

76
00:06:28,060 --> 00:06:30,160
so that we are able to reproduce it.

77
00:06:33,500 --> 00:06:37,920
Looking in more detail at what MLflow is actually tracking:

78
00:06:38,560 --> 00:06:40,700
we have two experiments set up.

79
00:06:41,240 --> 00:06:45,140
The first one tracks each training run:

80
00:06:45,940 --> 00:06:50,080
which settings were used for XGBoost,

81
00:06:50,200 --> 00:06:53,620
the number of trees, the depth, the learning rate,

82
00:06:55,260 --> 00:06:59,280
which training rows and class weights were used,

83
00:06:59,380 --> 00:07:01,490
then how the model actually performs:

84
00:07:01,540 --> 00:07:05,160
the ROC AUC, the recall, the precision,

85
00:07:07,080 --> 00:07:11,080
and which data and which code were used,

86
00:07:11,500 --> 00:07:15,640
because then we can really reproduce everything.

87
00:07:16,340 --> 00:07:19,260
The artifacts: the model itself,

88
00:07:19,440 --> 00:07:23,150
the dataset manifest, and a JSON file that ties everything together,

89
00:07:23,200 --> 00:07:25,300
so it's reproducible.

90
00:07:27,340 --> 00:07:32,930
The second experiment we have is run

91
00:07:32,980 --> 00:07:37,960
every day, and it shows the Evidently report.

92
00:07:38,300 --> 00:07:41,060
So it's for drift detection.

93
00:07:43,400 --> 00:07:49,010
Thomas will explain more about these runs later, when he presents

94
00:07:49,060 --> 00:07:52,020
Airflow, so I won't go into more detail here.

95
00:07:54,380 --> 00:07:58,880
Looking at the models in more detail:

96
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:03,840
we start with a baseline model, which is the champion.

97
00:08:04,760 --> 00:08:07,900
If a new model is generated,

98
00:08:08,260 --> 00:08:13,730
we look at three criteria to know if it can replace the current champion,

99
00:08:13,780 --> 00:08:15,620
or if it's not better.

100
00:08:17,240 --> 00:08:19,310
The first requirement is that everything is there,

101
00:08:19,360 --> 00:08:20,560
everything is clean:

102
00:08:21,080 --> 00:08:22,980
all the files are in MLflow,

103
00:08:23,620 --> 00:08:26,060
so everything is reproducible.

104
00:08:27,060 --> 00:08:31,760
Then, the validation recall needs to be higher than 0.75.

105
00:08:32,640 --> 00:08:36,350
And the validation ROC AUC has to beat the champion.

106
00:08:36,400 --> 00:08:41,880
If those criteria are met, it can become champion.

107
00:08:42,100 --> 00:08:44,480
So the champion is not necessarily the newest model.

108
00:08:44,560 --> 00:08:46,660
It's the model that performs best.

109
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:53,420
FastAPI, which Thomas will also explain later,

110
00:08:54,320 --> 00:09:00,520
asks which model is currently the champion, and then serves that model.

111
00:09:02,950 --> 00:09:05,200
Thomas will explain more about this now,

112
00:09:05,250 --> 00:09:08,350
so I'll hand over to him.

113
00:09:09,130 --> 00:09:13,400
We built an API that loads its Basic authentication credentials from

114
00:09:13,450 --> 00:09:17,240
Supabase Vault. We have four public endpoints.

115
00:09:17,290 --> 00:09:21,140
Health is open, then predict takes one day of measurements,

116
00:09:21,190 --> 00:09:24,600
and live predict takes only a station name and predicts whether it's going

117
00:09:24,650 --> 00:09:28,380
to rain or not. The train endpoint retrains the model.

118
00:09:28,430 --> 00:09:32,040
We also have a fifth endpoint, the metrics endpoint.

119
00:09:32,090 --> 00:09:37,460
It's for Prometheus only, and Nginx answers with a 404 from outside,

120
00:09:37,510 --> 00:09:38,830
but more on that later.

121
00:09:39,570 --> 00:09:41,290
We have input bounds.

122
00:09:41,630 --> 00:09:46,690
For example, rainfall has to be between 0 and 500 millimeters,

123
00:09:47,050 --> 00:09:50,300
and unknown stations are rejected with a suggestion for likely typos.

124
00:09:50,350 --> 00:09:54,300
Live predict looks up the station's coordinates,

125
00:09:54,350 --> 00:09:59,280
fetches today's weather from Open-Meteo with a 30-second timeout, and returns a

126
00:09:59,330 --> 00:10:02,490
502 if the data is not available yet.

127
00:10:02,790 --> 00:10:05,980
Train accepts five hyperparameters with bounds,

128
00:10:06,030 --> 00:10:11,700
for example a depth of up to 15, and returns metrics on the training and

129
00:10:11,750 --> 00:10:14,630
validation data, and clears the prediction cache.

130
00:10:15,170 --> 00:10:17,620
Every answer includes the model that served it:

131
00:10:17,670 --> 00:10:20,350
its name, alias, version and source.

132
00:10:20,730 --> 00:10:24,380
Every prediction is also logged to Supabase with its features,

133
00:10:24,430 --> 00:10:27,680
result, latency and model.

134
00:10:27,730 --> 00:10:30,310
Later, when we know the real weather,

135
00:10:30,690 --> 00:10:35,040
we can measure how good the model really was, but more on that in

136
00:10:35,090 --> 00:10:39,160
monitoring. The API checks the champion alias on each prediction,

137
00:10:39,210 --> 00:10:42,950
so a promotion or rollback takes effect without a restart.

138
00:10:44,030 --> 00:10:45,880
We have two DAGs.

139
00:10:45,930 --> 00:10:50,640
The weather pipeline runs every day at 18:00 Berlin time.

140
00:10:50,690 --> 00:10:56,640
It fetches new weather data, cleans it, adds the real outcome to past

141
00:10:56,690 --> 00:10:59,540
predictions, and runs the Evidently monitoring job.

142
00:10:59,590 --> 00:11:03,730
Then we have the weather retrain DAG, which has no schedule.

143
00:11:03,910 --> 00:11:07,280
It trains, validates and compares with the champion.

144
00:11:07,330 --> 00:11:12,920
Grafana starts it through the Airflow REST API when the real recall drops.

145
00:11:12,970 --> 00:11:17,070
For this, the Airflow web server joins our Docker network.

146
00:11:17,750 --> 00:11:20,550
So why do we split those tasks?

147
00:11:20,750 --> 00:11:22,990
Checking is cheap, but training is not,

148
00:11:23,070 --> 00:11:27,610
and retraining on a fixed schedule would train even when nothing is wrong.

149
00:11:27,950 --> 00:11:33,440
Each task is a DockerOperator that runs on our images, and containers are

150
00:11:33,490 --> 00:11:35,140
removed after each task.

151
00:11:35,190 --> 00:11:40,860
The train task calls POST /train through Nginx, like

152
00:11:40,910 --> 00:11:43,410
any client, so the stack must be up.

153
00:11:43,850 --> 00:11:49,040
Airflow reaches Docker through a socket proxy that allows containers, images

154
00:11:49,090 --> 00:11:53,230
and POST calls, and denies network management for safety.

155
00:11:53,710 --> 00:11:58,190
Each task retries up to three times, one minute apart.

156
00:11:58,350 --> 00:12:01,200
If Airflow was down, it catches up every missed day,

157
00:12:01,250 --> 00:12:05,900
one run at a time, and each run fetches the weather of its own day.

158
00:12:05,950 --> 00:12:09,240
For the days that were already missing,

159
00:12:09,290 --> 00:12:11,370
we ran a one-time backfill.

160
00:12:11,930 --> 00:12:17,160
A retrained model only becomes champion if it passes the recall gate and beats

161
00:12:17,210 --> 00:12:18,530
the current champion.

162
00:12:19,830 --> 00:12:22,390
We also built continuous integration and delivery.

163
00:12:22,990 --> 00:12:25,820
The first line is our continuous integration.

164
00:12:25,870 --> 00:12:29,140
CI runs on every push and pull request to master, in two jobs.

165
00:12:29,190 --> 00:12:34,440
Job 1 is Ruff linting and a format check, then the unit tests.

166
00:12:34,490 --> 00:12:39,620
Job 2, in Docker, builds all seven images, starts the API and Nginx, and runs

167
00:12:39,670 --> 00:12:41,960
the live gateway tests against them.

168
00:12:42,010 --> 00:12:43,810
The second line is our release.

169
00:12:44,190 --> 00:12:48,100
Every time code lands on master, GitHub builds our ingestion,

170
00:12:48,150 --> 00:12:53,930
preprocess and API images, and uploads them to the GitHub Container Registry.

171
00:12:54,090 --> 00:12:59,030
Any server can then download and start them without building them itself.

172
00:13:00,270 --> 00:13:05,640
For monitoring, we use Prometheus and Grafana, and we have two kinds of

173
00:13:05,690 --> 00:13:09,700
monitoring: live monitoring from the API, and the daily batch check.

174
00:13:09,750 --> 00:13:13,040
First, let's talk about the live monitoring.

175
00:13:13,090 --> 00:13:14,930
The API measures itself.

176
00:13:15,070 --> 00:13:18,960
Every request is counted by endpoint and status, and timed.

177
00:13:19,010 --> 00:13:24,260
On top, live predictions record the station, rain or no rain, the probability and the

178
00:13:24,310 --> 00:13:27,650
model that answered. Training records the validation metrics.

179
00:13:28,030 --> 00:13:31,420
All of it is on the metrics endpoint.

180
00:13:31,470 --> 00:13:36,860
As I said before, Nginx blocks it from outside, and Prometheus reads it

181
00:13:36,910 --> 00:13:40,560
inside the Docker network every 15 seconds.

182
00:13:40,610 --> 00:13:45,000
Once a day, when the real weather is known, an Evidently job runs two checks.

183
00:13:45,050 --> 00:13:49,910
First, it checks whether the inputs have changed compared to the training data.

184
00:13:50,130 --> 00:13:54,460
We call this feature drift, and it only raises a warning, because changed inputs

185
00:13:54,510 --> 00:13:57,030
do not prove the model got worse.

186
00:13:57,110 --> 00:14:00,070
Second, it checks whether the model was actually right.

187
00:14:00,330 --> 00:14:04,480
As soon as at least 30 predictions have a known outcome,

188
00:14:04,530 --> 00:14:06,710
it computes the real recall.

189
00:14:06,790 --> 00:14:11,610
If recall is below 0.75, it requests a retrain.

190
00:14:12,230 --> 00:14:15,280
Evidently is a batch job that ends after a few seconds,

191
00:14:15,330 --> 00:14:19,380
so Prometheus cannot scrape it. It pushes its numbers to a Pushgateway

192
00:14:19,430 --> 00:14:22,110
instead, and Prometheus reads them from there.

193
00:14:22,730 --> 00:14:27,240
Grafana turns the retrain request into an alert and calls Airflow through a

194
00:14:27,290 --> 00:14:32,270
webhook, at most once a day, and that starts the weather retrain DAG.

195
00:14:32,490 --> 00:14:33,640
We have seven alerts.

196
00:14:33,690 --> 00:14:38,300
For the API: API down, more than 10% server errors, and

197
00:14:38,350 --> 00:14:42,260
average latency above 5 seconds. For the model:

198
00:14:42,310 --> 00:14:48,100
a retrain request, last training below ROC AUC 0.866 or recall

199
00:14:48,150 --> 00:14:53,310
under 0.75, and an unusual rain rate.

200
00:14:53,890 --> 00:14:58,780
Only the retrain request starts training. The others are warnings for a

201
00:14:58,830 --> 00:15:02,230
person, for now only visible in Grafana.

202
00:15:02,470 --> 00:15:05,220
Our dashboards and alerts are not clicked together by hand.

203
00:15:05,270 --> 00:15:07,740
They are stored as files in Git,

204
00:15:07,790 --> 00:15:12,540
so anyone on the team can rebuild the same monitoring with one command.

205
00:15:12,590 --> 00:15:15,630
Now I will hand over to Ziad.

206
00:15:15,820 --> 00:15:23,960
I will leave the slides and show the system doing two or three things.

207
00:15:23,960 --> 00:15:27,500
One is a forecast, a live prediction, and one is a training run.

208
00:15:27,500 --> 00:15:32,880
Both go through the same API code.

209
00:15:32,880 --> 00:15:35,160
This is the live prediction.

210
00:15:35,160 --> 00:15:38,701
I picked Sydney since it's chosen at the moment,

211
00:15:38,701 --> 00:15:41,800
and I asked whether it will rain tomorrow

212
00:15:41,800 --> 00:15:49,000
using the live weather feed by pressing the predict live weather.

213
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:56,400
Now, it predicted yes with a probability of 95%.

214
00:15:56,400 --> 00:16:02,360
The 95% comes from our model, basically, this is the probability of our model.

215
00:16:02,360 --> 00:16:06,140
And the answer stays on the screen right now, and there's either rain or no.

216
00:16:06,140 --> 00:16:09,120
Here in this case is yes, and we have the probability as I said.

217
00:16:09,120 --> 00:16:15,160
This is the result after the serving API returns.

218
00:16:15,160 --> 00:16:22,380
The call goes through Nginx, like the rest of the API users.

219
00:16:22,380 --> 00:16:26,852
Under the forecast here, we can see that these two panels,

220
00:16:26,852 --> 00:16:29,320
which are real Grafana renders,

221
00:16:29,320 --> 00:16:33,280
those are charts for the last five minutes right now.

222
00:16:33,280 --> 00:16:38,380
So we took this time snapshot, and they refresh every five seconds.

223
00:16:38,380 --> 00:16:41,727
So one is the number of requests on the left-hand side,

224
00:16:41,727 --> 00:16:44,040
and others how long those calls took.

225
00:16:44,040 --> 00:16:47,231
The prediction we just made shows up here,

226
00:16:47,231 --> 00:16:52,700
so we can see that the API actually hits, which is here the blue graph.

227
00:16:52,700 --> 00:16:54,520
This happened right now.

228
00:16:54,520 --> 00:17:02,700
And then we can see the duration, which took 3.27 seconds.

229
00:17:02,700 --> 00:17:07,780
Now, so we can see that the API actually works.

230
00:17:07,780 --> 00:17:12,880
And it will stay on these two panels.

231
00:17:12,880 --> 00:17:15,420
The second action is the model training.

232
00:17:15,420 --> 00:17:20,964
So we have a, through the same path, through Nginx and our API calls,

233
00:17:20,964 --> 00:17:24,820
we have a train endpoint, which we can run now.

234
00:17:24,820 --> 00:17:29,211
And pressing this button, which train model,

235
00:17:29,211 --> 00:17:35,000
now sends one training request through that same gateway.

236
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:41,360
Before it first fits the model, the system checks the DVC raw data.

237
00:17:41,360 --> 00:17:47,090
If that data is newer than the processed split, it merges the raw files

238
00:17:47,090 --> 00:17:52,580
with the available ones, rebuilds the splits, and pre-processes.

239
00:17:52,580 --> 00:17:59,500
The splits we have now matches the newest raw data.

240
00:17:59,500 --> 00:18:04,460
So this run only retrained, then reports the metrics.

241
00:18:04,460 --> 00:18:08,272
As you can see right now, we got a completed training,

242
00:18:08,272 --> 00:18:10,460
which took around 2.3 seconds.

243
00:18:10,460 --> 00:18:16,540
And we have the validation scores, the recall and ROC-AUC validation scores.

244
00:18:16,540 --> 00:18:19,800
Now, one thing to notice here, it checked that the

245
00:18:19,800 --> 00:18:23,320
splits is already on the freshest raw data basically.

246
00:18:23,320 --> 00:18:27,320
And we trained on that split basically, then without resplitting.

